00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:04.245
So let's start with why.

00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:09.590
Big data is in
our DNA at Google.

00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:11.950
We have over eight products--

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:14.803
so we have eight products
with over 1 billion users.

00:01:14.803 --> 00:01:17.470
A lot of the products that we're
making available through to you

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:20.625
to do these types of problems--
to solve these problems,

00:01:20.625 --> 00:01:22.000
are things that
we use internally

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:26.060
and that we have made
available to the community.

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:28.150
So this was started
in 2002, where

00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:30.620
we've been innovating on
lots of different products.

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:35.500
So you might have heard
of Bigtable or Spanner.

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:38.380
And a lot of these
innovations were

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:41.660
made available to the
open source community.

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:45.550
And so what we're now doing
is then bringing those back

00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:47.500
to Google Cloud
Platform and making

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:51.030
them available as services--
as managed services to you.

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:55.060
Things like BigQuery
and Pub/Sub and Bigtable

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:57.970
are all services
that we provide.

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:04.790
So our focus for the customer
is to focus on insights.

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:08.060
So rather than you having to
worry about the infrastructure,

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:10.770
letting you focus on what
you want to do with the data

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:15.120
and get the insights
out is our key focus.

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:17.390
Another is looking at
streaming analytics.

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:20.270
So being able to get real
time business insights

00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:22.800
and make your business
more responsive.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.140
So if you can get
insights in real time,

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:27.480
then you can also
react in real time.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:31.560
And then we make that easier
to do using machine learning.

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:34.063
So what you can
do is, once you've

00:02:34.063 --> 00:02:35.480
got your data
coming in real time,

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:37.660
apply our machine learning
algorithm on top of that

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:41.060
and actually make it
actionable automatically

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:45.830
without you having to
manually intervene.

00:02:45.830 --> 00:02:47.760
So I'll talk about what next.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:49.640
So what's available on
Google Cloud Platform

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:54.260
to do streaming analytics
and streaming apps.

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:57.810
So we focus on four
stages of a data platform.

00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:01.418
So the first is the ability
to ingest at any scale.

00:03:01.418 --> 00:03:02.960
So what we're going
to show you today

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:04.850
is actually not that
we can do streaming,

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:06.270
but that we can do it at scale.

00:03:06.270 --> 00:03:08.590
So we're going to
have the ability

00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:12.780
to ingest thousands of
millions of messages.

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:17.480
The second is to process and
transform the data reliably.

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:20.330
The third is to
store seamlessly.

00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:26.240
And the fourth is to analyze,
learn, visualize, with speed.

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:28.310
So we're going to go
through each one of these

00:03:28.310 --> 00:03:30.770
and I'm going to talk through
all the Google Cloud Platform

00:03:30.770 --> 00:03:33.642
products that enable
each of these.

00:03:33.642 --> 00:03:34.850
So that brings me to the how.

00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:40.560
This is a typical data pipeline
that I going to walk through.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:42.685
And we have lots of different
ways of doing things,

00:03:42.685 --> 00:03:45.770
so this is just, sort of, one
way of piecing things together,

00:03:45.770 --> 00:03:49.720
but it's quite a
common architecture.

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:53.670
So we start with
streaming events coming in

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:55.630
from IoT devices,
or in this case

00:03:55.630 --> 00:03:57.880
we're actually going to have
mobile device data that's

00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:01.920
coming in and that will be
written to a Cloud Pub/Sub

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:02.710
topic.

00:04:02.710 --> 00:04:06.330
And we can read from that
Cloud Pub/Sub topic as well.

00:04:06.330 --> 00:04:08.550
So Cloud Pub/Sub as is a
serverless, fully managed

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:11.140
asynchronous messaging platform.

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:14.020
So basically it's
pretty straightforward.

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:16.180
You just publish a
topic and read from it.

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:18.790
The second is, we can
also ingest batch data.

00:04:18.790 --> 00:04:21.040
So you can actually do both
of those at the same time.

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:23.960
So Dataflow, which is
going to be the next step,

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:27.330
is going to be able to handle
both those types of streams

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:29.670
of data sources.

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:32.190
And that is going to be read
into Cloud Storage, which

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:36.850
is our performant, unified and
cost-effective object storage

00:04:36.850 --> 00:04:41.020
bucket for binary blob data.

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:43.050
So then it's going to
go into Cloud Dataflow.

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:46.860
So Dataflow has connectors to
Pub/Sub and to Cloud Storage.

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:49.140
And it's going to be
able to process our data

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:54.060
and apply transformations as we
need them to batch your stream.

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:56.480
So one interesting
thing about Dataflow

00:04:56.480 --> 00:04:58.490
is that we can handle
real time streaming data.

00:04:58.490 --> 00:04:59.880
So we can apply things--

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:01.740
something you do
differently to batch data

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:04.080
is you apply things like
windowing functions, where

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:07.110
you take an aggregate of the
data that's being read in

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:11.630
and you pass that downstream.

00:05:11.630 --> 00:05:14.120
But we can also use all
the products like Dataproc.

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:16.670
So, if you have already
got Spark and Hadoop jobs,

00:05:16.670 --> 00:05:21.100
you can migrate them
over to Dataproc.

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:23.560
And we can then, from
Dataflow, write it out

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:25.180
to be BigQuery or Bigtable.

00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:26.930
So it can go to lots
of different sources,

00:05:26.930 --> 00:05:29.050
this is just an example.

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:32.040
So BigQuery is our Data
Warehousing solution

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:34.990
and Bigtable is our
high performance NoSQL

00:05:34.990 --> 00:05:38.790
database for large workloads.

00:05:38.790 --> 00:05:41.430
And then we can apply machine
learning on top of that.

00:05:41.430 --> 00:05:44.240
So once the data is in the
database, we can pull it out

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:46.520
or we can actually use it
directly from Dataflow.

00:05:48.912 --> 00:05:50.870
And then we have a lot
of visualization options

00:05:50.870 --> 00:05:52.230
and different analytical tools.

00:05:52.230 --> 00:05:53.780
So we can use Cloud
Datalab law which

00:05:53.780 --> 00:05:56.970
